MemSolve retains BERT projection biases and its FFN output residual module.

The sigmoid output gate follows the placement/activation studied in
Gated Attention (Qiu et al., 2025): https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.06708
https://github.com/qiuzh20/gated_attention/blob/main/modeling_qwen3.py
Post-RMS placement also follows FLA Gated DeltaNet (which uses SiLU).
The low-rank linear factorization is MemSolve's cost choice. The fused
post-RMS gate follows FLA's norm-before-gate forward/backward decomposition,
with sigmoid rather than SiLU and FP32 normalization/gating arithmetic:
https://github.com/fla-org/flash-linear-attention/blob/8024667ab58fdd8986587147fca71fecc017f977/fla/modules/layernorm_gated.py
The three-residual BF16/FP32 readout product follows the multi-component
arithmetic construction of Henry et al. (2019), https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06376.
Since one operand is exactly BF16, three residual products suffice without
the cross terms required for two FP32 operands.

The FP16 readout-forward / BF16 backward storage policy is informed by
FLA Mesa's range-aware Q/K precision and its state/small-gradient fixes:
https://github.com/fla-org/flash-linear-attention/pull/1152
https://github.com/fla-org/flash-linear-attention/issues/1283
