The NLP experiment entrypoints use Hugging Face Datasets/Transformers,
Sentence Transformers and MTEB through their public APIs. The MLM recipe is
adapted from LIONS-EPFL/LION (Masked_Language_Modeling/yamls/pretrain), with
Composer-to-PyTorch weight-decay conversion. Retrieval data/loss choices follow
JHU-CLSP/ettin-encoder-vs-decoder, retrieval_eval/train_st.py (Apache-2.0,
Copyright 2024 onwards Answer.AI, LightOn, and contributors). These framework
implementations are imported, not vendored; adaptations are documented in
docs/BERT_MLM.md.

The compact triangular solves use NVIDIA cuSOLVERDx's TRSM device
operation and follow the cooperative load/solve/store structure in MathDx
26.06.1 example/cusolverdx/07_BLAS/trsm_batched_block.cu (Apache-2.0).
Copyright (c) 2026 NVIDIA CORPORATION & AFFILIATES. All rights reserved.
https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cusolverdx/get_started/functions/trsm.html

The compact FP32 Tensor Core products and bounded statistics scheduling draw
on FLA's tile organization and explicit product-precision choices (MIT):
https://github.com/fla-org/flash-linear-attention/blob/78a39922d56562336e22e5a429b039f34ef2bf7f/fla/ops/utils/solve_tril.py
https://github.com/fla-org/flash-linear-attention/blob/78a39922d56562336e22e5a429b039f34ef2bf7f/fla/ops/common/chunk_o.py
The projected GEMM tiles follow Triton's matrix-multiplication tutorial:
https://triton-lang.org/main/getting-started/tutorials/03-matrix-multiplication.html
These references inform algorithm/scheduling choices; MemSolve's equations
and analytic derivatives remain independently defined in reference.py.

Earlier revisions used NVIDIA cuSOLVERDx POTRS for complement; the current
common path uses POTRF/TRSM inverse factors. The projection weight/bias gradient calls the cuBLASLt BGRADB epilogue with FP32 accumulation.
These are library API integrations, not vendored solver or GEMM implementations.
https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cusolverdx/get_started/potrs.html
https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cublas/index.html#cublasltepilogue-t

Earlier compact factor/solve composition used the NVIDIA cuSOLVERDx POTRF,
TRSM and POTRS APIs. Its shared-map gradient used the split-reduction
organization described by CUTLASS. The current path reuses the already-needed
compact product instead; these algorithm attributions are retained.
cuBLASLt host-plan reuse follows NVIDIA's heuristics-cache guidance.
https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cusolverdx/get_started/posv.html
https://docs.nvidia.com/cutlass/latest/media/docs/cpp/efficient_gemm.html#parallelized-reductions
https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cublas/index.html#heuristics-cache

Earlier Cholesky-adjoint fusion composed the cuSOLVERDx left/transposed and
right/nontransposed TRSM operations cited above in shared memory. No solver
source is vendored. Compact-product epilogues follow the cited Triton
post-accumulation fusion pattern; the analytic VJP remains the repository's
reference contract.

The common inverse-factor CUDA path reuses the cuSOLVERDx POTRF/TRSM APIs
above and is informed by selective triangular inversion:
Wicky, Solomonik, Hoefler (IPDPS 2017), https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.01855.
The compact adjoint follows Cholesky differentiation (Murray, 2016,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.07527) with MemSolve-specific common-subexpression
reuse. No solver source from these papers is vendored.
Exact sequence statistics use PyTorch/NCCL SUM collectives; convolution halos
use PyTorch's autograd-enabled functional all_gather_single. Parameter gradients
use SUM across sequence shards, following PyTorch's sequence-parallel examples:
https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/torch/distributed/_functional_collectives.py
https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/test/distributed/tensor/parallel/test_tp_examples.py
Compact scalar precision: NVIDIA libdevice tanh/log1p are called through
Triton's supported external-function interface; no library implementation is
copied. The tanh value and its analytic exponential derivative are evaluated
separately to retain both small values and representable tail gradients.
https://triton-lang.org/main/getting-started/tutorials/07-extern-functions.html

MLM forward/loss/backward graph capture reuses PyTorch make_graphed_callables:
https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.cuda.make_graphed_callables.html
Independent accumulation buffers address the upstream static-gradient alias:
https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/181723
HF Trainer/Accelerate continues to own DDP, loss normalization, optimization
and checkpoint lifecycle; no PyTorch graph implementation is vendored.
